---
// The quick start at the top of the Guide (#415 (new site with the book)):
// install, the first test, run it, read the report, replay a failing seed.
// The test is the crate-level doc test of `moonpool`, read at build time, so
// it compiles against the current API. The report is one real run of it.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro";
import { url } from "../../../nav";
import { builderCall, moonpoolDocTest, workspaceVersion } from "../../../lib/source.mjs";
import { ROOT } from "../../../lib/book.mjs";

const test = moonpoolDocTest();
const call = builderCall(test);
const items = test.slice(0, test.indexOf(call)).trimEnd();
const testFile = `${items}

#[test]
fn server_accepts_under_chaos() -> Result<(), moonpool::SimulationError> {
${call
  .replace("assert_eq!(report.failed_runs, 0);", "report.eprint();\nassert_eq!(report.failed_runs, 0);")
  .split("\n")
  .map((l) => "    " + l)
  .join("\n")}
    Ok(())
}`;
const version = workspaceVersion();
const report = readFileSync(ROOT + "website/site/src/samples/quick-start-report.txt", "utf8").trimEnd();
const base = url();
---
<Guide
  title="Quick start"
  here="guide/quick-start/"
  description="Add moonpool, write one test, run it, read the report, replay a seed."
  prev={{ title: "The Sim Book", path: "guide/" }}
  next={{ title: "Guide for AI Agents", path: "llms.html" }}
>
  <h2 id="install">1. Add moonpool</h2>
  <p>Add the two crates to the <code>[dev-dependencies]</code> of your crate. The default features of
    <code>moonpool</code> give the simulator and the Tokio providers.</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="toml" code={`[dev-dependencies]\nmoonpool = "${version}"\nasync-trait = "0.1"`} />

  <h2 id="the-first-test">2. Write the first test</h2>
  <p>Put this file in <code>tests/chaos.rs</code>. <code>Server</code> is the system under test: a
    <code>Process</code> that the simulator boots again after each reboot. <code>Client</code> is the test driver: a
    <code>Workload</code> that survives the reboots and judges the run. The builder runs ten seeds with network
    faults on.</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="rust" code={testFile} />

  <h2 id="run-it">3. Run it</h2>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="sh" code="cargo test --test chaos -- --nocapture" />
  <p>Ten seeds run in milliseconds of wall time. Each seed simulates a few seconds: the clock jumps to the
    next event, so no test waits.</p>

  <h2 id="read-the-report">4. Read the report</h2>
  <p><code>report.eprint()</code> writes the report to stderr. This is one real run. The seeds and the wall
    times change from run to run.</p>
  <pre><code>{report}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>✓ 100.0%</strong>: no seed failed, panicked or stopped.</li>
    <li><strong>Base seed</strong>: the seed of the whole campaign. <code>.base_seed(…)</code> runs the same ten seeds again.</li>
    <li><strong>always</strong> with <strong>0 fail</strong>: the invariant held each time.</li>
    <li><strong>sometimes</strong> and <strong>reachable</strong> with ✓: the rare path fired at least once. The
      <code>network:</code> rows come from the simulator: they show that the faults happened.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="replay-a-seed">5. Replay a failing seed</h2>
  <p>When a seed fails, the report names it under <strong>Faulty seeds</strong> and prints the line to replay
    it. Run that seed alone:</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="rust" code={`SimulationBuilder::new()\n    .processes(1, || Box::new(Server))\n    .workload(Client)\n    .enable_chaos([Chaos::Network(ChaosMode::Swarm)])\n    .set_debug_seeds(vec![7891])\n    .set_iterations(1)\n    .run()?;`} />
  <p>The same seed gives the same run: the same order of the tasks, the same latency for each message, the
    same faults. Raise the log level and read the trace back from the first wrong event.</p>

  <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={base + "part3-building/01-first-simulation.html"}>Your First Simulation</a> builds a test step by step.</li>
    <li><a href={base + "part3-building/05-running.html"}>Running and Observing</a> explains each part of the report.</li>
    <li><a href={base + "part3-building/20-debugging.html"}>Debugging a Failing Seed</a> goes from a red seed to the bug.</li>
    <li><a href={base + "part4-integration/05-production.html"}>Using Providers in Production</a> runs the same code on Tokio.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
